    model: 'High glucose and high blood pressure damage the small blood vessels of the kidneys over years, thickening and scarring the glomeruli so they filter less blood; his eGFR of 18 means about a fifth of normal filtration remains. Because the kidneys cannot remove nitrogen wastes, urea and other toxins accumulate (uremia), causing fatigue, nausea and itching. They cannot excrete enough water and sodium, so fluid builds up, causing ankle edema and worsening blood pressure. They also cannot excrete potassium, so it rises to 6.1 mEq/L, which can cause dangerous heart rhythms. Damaged kidneys make less erythropoietin, so his bone marrow makes fewer red blood cells, causing anemia and more fatigue. For end-stage kidney disease, options are hemodialysis (usually three times a week through an AV fistula, which must be protected from blood pressure cuffs and needle sticks), peritoneal dialysis at home, or a kidney transplant, along with limits on potassium, phosphorus, sodium and fluid, and treatment of anemia.',
